  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "A simple random sample of 2 items is selected from a population of 10 items. How many different selections are possible?",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "45",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Method 1: Count both orders\n\nA sample consists of two distinct items, and order does not matter. There are \\(10\\) choices for a first item and \\(9\\) for a different second item, giving \\(10\\times9\\) ordered pairs. Every sample appears exactly twice, once in each order, so the number of combinations is\n\\[\nC(10,2)=\\frac{10\\times9}{2}=45.\n\\]\n\nMethod 2: Count by the smaller label\n\nLabel the items \\(1,2,\\ldots,10\\). Count each pair according to its smaller label: label \\(1\\) has \\(9\\) possible larger partners, label \\(2\\) has \\(8\\), and so on down to label \\(9\\), which has \\(1\\). Every two-item selection has exactly one smaller label, so these cases include every selection exactly once. Thus the number of selections is\n\\[\n9+8+7+6+5+4+3+2+1=45.\n\\]",
